The strongest reason your explanation is wrong is that `Connect Wire` is not a reliable ?�replace this existing input connection??operation. You invoked it on already-wired sinks. Your project?�s own verified contract says that this case can reroute and break the diagram, while branching from an already-wired source is supported ([vi-scripting.md](<G:/Codes/LabVIEW_Codes/MinLab/zz_LabView VI/AAA_UNIST/2. Tracking/V6_ParallelLoop/.agents/skills/labview-automation/references/vi-scripting.md>), [gscript.py](<G:/Codes/LabVIEW_Codes/MinLab/zz_LabView VI/AAA_UNIST/2. Tracking/V6_ParallelLoop/tools/gscript.py:2084>)). Therefore wire 1208/1444 is not evidence that the intended rewire succeeded.

1. Already-wired destination

NI?�s public description only says `Terminal.Connect Wire` connects a wire to the terminal; it does not specify an atomic replace/no-op contract for an already-wired sink ([Connect Wire API](https://labviewwiki.org/wiki/Terminal_class/Connect_Wire_method)).

The distinctions are:

- Wired source ??additional unwired sink: a branch is valid.
- Different source ??already-wired sink: this is not a branch. A valid LabVIEW wire has one source; a wire containing multiple sources is broken ([NI wire model](https://www.ni.com/docs/en-US/bundle/labview/page/using-wires-to-link-block-diagram-objects.html), [Wire.Terminals[]](https://labviewwiki.org/wiki/Wire_class/Terminals%28%29_property)).
- Your locally measured behavior is ?�reroutes and breaks,??not dependable replacement and not dependable no-op.

H2 is already falsified as a universal explanation: node 482 changed from 751 to 1444. At least that call did something.

For deterministic replacement, first capture the old wire?�s source and all sinks, delete/reconstruct the affected topology, then connect only unwired sinks. An NI example recommends exactly this delete-and-reconnect-all-other-sinks approach when changing one branch ([NI Community](https://forums.ni.com/t5/LabVIEW/Disconnect-wire-through-VI-scripting/m-p/3046935)).

2. Wire identity and branching

H1?�s ?�connecting always creates a new wire??is false.

A normal fan-out is one wire object with one source and several sinks. `Wire.Terminals[]` returns all terminals connected by that wire, with the source first when one exists ([Wire.Terminals[]](https://labviewwiki.org/wiki/Wire_class/Terminals%28%29_property)). NI likewise defines a wire as having one source but potentially many readers ([NI wire model](https://www.ni.com/docs/en-US/bundle/labview/page/using-wires-to-link-block-diagram-objects.html)).

Consequently:

- A successful branch of wire 1081 should normally preserve wire UID 1081.
- UIDs remain associated with the same object; a new UID means a different wire object was created, or the original was deleted/replaced ([GObject.UID](https://labviewwiki.org/wiki/GObject_class/UID_property)).
- R1 producing 1208 therefore was not merely ??081 extended normally.??- Because node 241?�s destination was already wired, B2?�s precondition was bad?�but that does not make the build correct. It means B2 detected an uncontrolled replacement/reroute rather than a clean branch.

3. `Owner` unwired while node 482 sees 1444

There is no need to declare `Terminal.Connected Wire` unreliable. The simpler alternative is that 1444 is a dangling or otherwise broken wire with node 482 as a sink but no valid source.

LabVIEW permits broken wire objects, and `Wire.Terminals[]` explicitly accounts for wires with no source or multiple sources ([NI broken wires](https://www.ni.com/docs/en-US/bundle/labview/page/using-wires-to-link-block-diagram-objects.html), [Wire.Terminals[]](https://labviewwiki.org/wiki/Wire_class/Terminals%28%29_property)). Thus ??82 reports wire 1444??does not imply ??444 must originate at node 241.??
Other viable explanations are:

- `connect2` created a source-less fragment at 482.
- It connected to a stale or newly regenerated Property-node terminal reference.
- The name lookup selected a different terminal after Property-node adaptation.
- Wires 751 and 1444 represent partial, inconsistent results of the three calls.

The observation that would falsify this alternative is: wire 1444?�s `Terminals[]` contains node 241?�s current `Owner` terminal with `Is Source? = TRUE`, plus all three consumer reference terminals. Your present terminal-level readings strongly predict that it will not.

4. Property-node class adaptation

Yes, changing the reference class can invalidate property items that the new class does not support. NI states that a Property Node?�s available properties change with the wired reference type ([NI dynamic Property Node example](https://knowledge.ni.com/KnowledgeArticleDetails?id=kA00Z0000019MTHSA2&l=en-US)); relinking to incompatible types has also been observed to leave an existing item invalid until it is selected again ([NI Community example](https://forums.ni.com/t5/LabVIEW/Changing-the-Property-Node-label-does-not-update-the-Terminal/td-p/1880343)).

But `Owner` itself is defined on the root `Generic` class, so it should remain semantically available for ordinary VI Server objects ([Generic.Owner](https://labviewwiki.org/wiki/Generic_class/Owner_property)). What is not documented is that terminal references or indices remain stable while a dynamic Property Node retypes.

Therefore refresh after R1:

- `Property Items[]` or `Properties[]`
- `Terminals[]`
- terminal name
- `Is Source?`
- data type
- connected-wire UID

Do not reuse a terminal index/reference captured before the reference class changed. For Property Nodes, the fixed first four terminals are reference-in, reference-out, error-in, error-out; property-item terminals follow them ([NI Community terminal analysis](https://forums.ni.com/t5/LabVIEW/Scripting-Terminal-Information/td-p/4303374)).

